{
  "annotations": {
    "list": []
  },
  "description": "The Litmus experiment timeline with the Kafka cluster and the Kates benchmark run laid over it: what was injected, what the brokers did, and what the workload underneath actually experienced.",
  "editable": true,
  "graphTooltip": 1,
  "panels": [
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "ChaosEngine custom resources currently in the `initialized` state \u2014 a fault is being applied right now. This is the panel that decides how to read every other panel on the board: a broker restart with this above zero is the experiment working, and the same restart with this at zero is an incident. The same distinction drives `KafkaBrokerRestartUnexpected` in prometheus-chaos-rules.yaml. Needs kube-state-metrics with custom-resource metrics enabled for ChaosEngine; without that configuration the series does not exist and the `or vector(0)` draws a reassuring zero, which is the one dishonest thing on this board and the reason it is said here.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              }
            ]
          },
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 4,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 1,
      "options": {
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "area",
        "justifyMode": "auto",
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "textMode": "auto"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "count(kube_customresource_chaosengine_status_engine_status{namespace=\"$namespace\", status=\"initialized\"} == 1) or vector(0)",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Chaos engines running",
      "type": "stat"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "Cumulative count of chaos experiments whose hypothesis held, from the Litmus chaos-exporter. Cumulative since the exporter started, so the useful reading is the change across a GameDay rather than the absolute number. Passed means Litmus' own probes were satisfied; it says nothing about what the Kates run underneath observed, which is the bottom row of this board.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              }
            ]
          },
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 4,
        "x": 4,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 2,
      "options": {
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "area",
        "justifyMode": "auto",
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "textMode": "auto"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum(litmuschaos_passed_experiments) or vector(0)",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Experiments passed",
      "type": "stat"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "The same counter for experiments whose hypothesis did not hold. A failure here is not necessarily a cluster problem: Litmus marks an experiment failed when it could not inject the fault at all, which is a chaos-tooling problem and means the resilience claim for that window is unsupported rather than disproved. `ChaosExperimentFailed` alerts on this.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": 1
              }
            ]
          },
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 4,
        "x": 8,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 3,
      "options": {
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "area",
        "justifyMode": "auto",
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "textMode": "auto"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum(litmuschaos_failed_experiments) or vector(0)",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Experiments failed",
      "type": "stat"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "The mean of Litmus' per-experiment probe success percentage. Probes are the experiment's own assertions \u2014 an HTTP check, a command, a Prometheus query \u2014 so this is Litmus grading itself, and it is averaged across every experiment the exporter knows about, including ones that finished hours ago. Below 50% raises `ChaosProbeSuccessLow`. Treat it as a hint to open the Litmus UI, not as a measurement of the cluster.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "color": {
            "mode": "thresholds"
          },
          "max": 100,
          "min": 0,
          "thresholds": {
            "mode": "absolute",
            "steps": [
              {
                "color": "red",
                "value": null
              },
              {
                "color": "yellow",
                "value": 50
              },
              {
                "color": "green",
                "value": 90
              }
            ]
          },
          "unit": "percent"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 4,
        "w": 4,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 0
      },
      "id": 4,
      "options": {
        "colorMode": "value",
        "graphMode": "area",
        "justifyMode": "auto",
        "reduceOptions": {
          "calcs": [
            "lastNotNull"
          ],
          "fields": "",
          "values": false
        },
        "textMode": "auto"
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "avg(litmuschaos_probe_success_percentage) or vector(0)",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Probe success rate",
      "type": "stat"
    },
    {
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 4
      },
      "id": 5,
      "panels": [],
      "title": "Kafka under fault",
      "type": "row"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "The `Ready` condition per pod, 1 or 0, from kube-state-metrics. During a pod-delete experiment this is the fault itself becoming visible, and the width of the trough is how long Kubernetes took to get the broker back \u2014 which is *not* RTO. RTO is how long the clients took to recover, and it is not measured anywhere in this repository; see the collapsed row at the bottom. A pod that never comes back raises `KafkaClusterNotReadyPostChaos`.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "decimals": 0,
          "max": 1,
          "min": 0,
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 5
      },
      "id": 6,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum by (pod) (kube_pod_status_ready{namespace=\"$namespace\", pod=~\"$pod\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{pod}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Broker pod readiness",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "Container restarts in the last five minutes, per pod. Under an active experiment this is expected; outside one it is `KafkaBrokerRestartUnexpected`, which is the alert the whole chaos rule file is built around. `increase()` over a counter that resets when the pod is replaced undercounts across a delete-and-recreate \u2014 the right direction to be wrong in, since it cannot report restarts that did not happen.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 5
      },
      "id": 7,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum by (pod) (increase(kube_pod_container_status_restarts_total{namespace=\"$namespace\", pod=~\"$pod\"}[5m]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{pod}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Broker restarts",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "CPU seconds per second, per broker pod, from cAdvisor. The shape to look for is *after* the fault clears: a broker that comes back and then sits at a much higher CPU than its peers is replaying log segments or catching up as a follower, and it is not really recovered yet even though its readiness probe passes. `KafkaHighCPUPostChaos` fires on exactly that above 0.8 cores with no experiment running.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "short"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 13
      },
      "id": 8,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum by (pod) (rate(container_cpu_usage_seconds_total{namespace=\"$namespace\", pod=~\"$pod\", container=\"kafka\"}[5m]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{pod}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Broker CPU cores",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "Container memory usage per broker pod, cAdvisor. Included because a memory-hog experiment is one of the faults Litmus injects and because a broker recovering from a delete rebuilds its page cache, which shows up here as a slow climb back to the pre-fault level. A broker whose memory never returns to that level has not finished recovering, whatever readiness says.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "bytes"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 12,
        "x": 12,
        "y": 13
      },
      "id": 9,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum by (pod) (container_memory_usage_bytes{namespace=\"$namespace\", pod=~\"$pod\", container=\"kafka\"})",
          "legendFormat": "{{pod}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Broker memory",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 21
      },
      "id": 10,
      "panels": [],
      "title": "Experiment history",
      "type": "row"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "How long each Litmus experiment ran, by chaos engine and chaos result \u2014 the chaos timeline this board is organised around. Line these steps up with the troughs in `Broker pod readiness` and the dips in the Kates row below: everything on this board is read relative to when the fault was being applied. This is the duration of the *injection*, not of the recovery.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "s"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 22
      },
      "id": 11,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "litmuschaos_experiment_total_duration",
          "legendFormat": "{{chaosengine_name}} \u2014 {{chaosresult_name}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Experiment duration",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "collapsed": false,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 30
      },
      "id": 12,
      "panels": [],
      "title": "Kates during chaos \u2014 the resilience evidence that works",
      "type": "row"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "What the Kates run underneath the experiment was actually achieving, records/second, per run. This is the resilience evidence: Litmus grades whether it injected the fault, and this panel grades whether the workload survived it. Throughput that dips and recovers is a cluster that failed over; throughput that goes to zero and stays there is one that did not. Empty means no benchmark was running during the window \u2014 a chaos experiment with no load under it proves nothing.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 8,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 31
      },
      "id": 13,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "kates_benchmark_throughput_rec_sec{job=\"$kates_job\"}",
          "legendFormat": "{{run_id}} ({{test_type}})",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Benchmark throughput during chaos",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "The run's p99, selected from the pre-computed percentile gauges the engine publishes \u2014 a `quantile` label to select, never `histogram_quantile()`. The interesting number is how high the spike goes and how long it lasts, because that is what a client of the real system would have experienced. A latency spike with no throughput dip is a failover the producers rode out; both together is an outage.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "ms"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 8,
        "x": 8,
        "y": 31
      },
      "id": 14,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "kates_benchmark_latency_ms{job=\"$kates_job\", quantile=\"0.99\"}",
          "legendFormat": "p99 {{phase}} {{run_id}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Benchmark p99 latency during chaos",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "datasource": {
        "type": "prometheus",
        "uid": "${datasource}"
      },
      "description": "Task failures per second in the run underneath the experiment. Errors here are the clearest statement that the fault reached the workload: not slower, actually failing. A pod-delete experiment that produces a latency spike and no errors is a cluster whose replication and client retries did their job, and that is the result the exercise is looking for.",
      "fieldConfig": {
        "defaults": {
          "custom": {
            "drawStyle": "line",
            "fillOpacity": 10,
            "lineWidth": 1,
            "showPoints": "never",
            "spanNulls": false
          },
          "unit": "ops"
        },
        "overrides": []
      },
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 8,
        "w": 8,
        "x": 16,
        "y": 31
      },
      "id": 15,
      "options": {
        "legend": {
          "calcs": [],
          "displayMode": "list",
          "placement": "bottom",
          "showLegend": true
        },
        "tooltip": {
          "mode": "multi",
          "sort": "desc"
        }
      },
      "targets": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "editorMode": "code",
          "expr": "sum by (run_id, phase) (rate(kates_benchmark_errors_total{job=\"$kates_job\"}[1m]))",
          "legendFormat": "{{run_id}} \u2014 {{phase}}",
          "range": true,
          "refId": "A"
        }
      ],
      "title": "Benchmark errors during chaos",
      "type": "timeseries"
    },
    {
      "collapsed": true,
      "gridPos": {
        "h": 1,
        "w": 24,
        "x": 0,
        "y": 39
      },
      "id": 16,
      "panels": [
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "description": "Prose, deliberately: the six panels below it cannot fill, and an operator opening this row during an incident has to be told that immediately rather than infer it from six blank tiles.",
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 6,
            "w": 24,
            "x": 0,
            "y": 40
          },
          "id": 17,
          "options": {
            "content": "**These six panels are empty, and not because the cluster is healthy.**\n\nThey read the `kafka:chaos:*` recording rules in `charts/monitoring/templates/prometheus-chaos-rules.yaml`. Those rules exist and are installed, but each one reads a `kates_integrity_result_*` or `kafka_chaos_*` series, and **nothing in this repository publishes any of them** \u2014 the integrity verifier computes RTO, RPO and data loss in Java and returns them over the REST API, but never registers them with Micrometer. A recording rule over a series that does not exist records nothing.\n\nThe same empty series carry four SLA alerts \u2014 `KafkaRTOExceedsSLA`, `KafkaRPOExceedsSLA`, `KafkaDataLossDetected`, `KafkaE2ELatencySpike` \u2014 which therefore install cleanly and can never fire.\n\nUntil an exporter publishes them, the resilience evidence on this board is the **Kates during chaos** row above: throughput, latency and errors observed by the workload while the fault was applied. `dashboards/kates-chaos/README.md` lists exactly what would have to be published for this row to work.",
            "mode": "markdown"
          },
          "targets": [],
          "title": "These six panels need an exporter this repository does not ship",
          "type": "text"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "description": "How long producers took to resume after the fault. Reads `kafka:chaos:producer_rto_seconds`, recorded from `kates_integrity_result_producer_rto_seconds`, which nothing publishes \u2014 see the panel above. The value exists inside the application as `IntegrityResult.producerRto` and reaches the REST API and the run report, but never Prometheus.",
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "color": {
                "mode": "thresholds"
              },
              "thresholds": {
                "mode": "absolute",
                "steps": [
                  {
                    "color": "green",
                    "value": null
                  },
                  {
                    "color": "red",
                    "value": 30
                  }
                ]
              },
              "unit": "s"
            },
            "overrides": []
          },
          "gridPos": {
            "h": 5,
            "w": 6,
            "x": 0,
            "y": 46
          },
          "id": 18,
          "options": {
            "colorMode": "value",
            "graphMode": "area",
            "justifyMode": "auto",
            "reduceOptions": {
              "calcs": [
                "lastNotNull"
              ],
              "fields": "",
              "values": false
            },
            "textMode": "auto"
          },
          "targets": [
            {
              "datasource": {
                "type": "prometheus",
                "uid": "${datasource}"
              },
              "editorMode": "code",
              "expr": "kafka:chaos:producer_rto_seconds",
              "range": true,
              "refId": "A"
            }
          ],
          "title": "Producer RTO",
          "type": "stat"
        },
        {
          "datasource": {
            "type": "prometheus",
            "uid": "${datasource}"
          },
          "description": "The same for consumers: how long before the consumer side was reading again. Reads `kafka:chaos:consumer_rto_seconds`, recorded from `kates_integrity_result_consumer_rto_seconds`, which nothing publishes. Producer and consumer RTO are tracked separately in `IntegrityResult` on purpose \u2014 a cluster can accept writes again well before its readers have caught up.",
          "fieldConfig": {
            "defaults": {
              "color": {
                "mode": "thresholds"
              },
